Results of Code Analyze
# Model Overview (for Diagnosis)

The probe requires the actual trained `NextItNetModel` at `view.model`. It 
accesses item embeddings and residual `NextItNetBlock` outputs through the host 
adapter. `view.log2feats(sequence)` supplies finite FP32 `B × L × H` features; 
the user representation is gathered at the **maximum nonzero sequence index**, 
correctly handling left padding. `view.predict_score` scores candidates. 
Backbone implementation details are not supplied here.

# Diagnosis Inputs & Hooks

`DiagnosisProbe(view, device)` reads training histories, category routing and 
batches `(None, sequence, positive, negative)`. It uniquely matches encoded 
histories and aligned targets to training endpoints, rejecting ambiguous 
matches. It selects at most 32 users, one event each, from ten batches. Each 
event receives its target plus up to 99 unique unseen alternatives, shared 
across conditions.

Diagnosis temporarily enables evaluation mode and deterministic FP32 execution, 
then restores modes, backend settings and RNG states.

# Probes & Metrics

- Outcomes are ``. Higher margins/hits and lower ranks indicate better target 
ranking.
- Summaries average draws within users, then weight users equally, reporting 
all/repeat/novel strata. Paired user-bootstrap intervals require at least ten 
users.
- Coverage, execution errors and numerical controls distinguish measured effects
from unavailable work.
- Separate callable helpers estimate embedding cosine similarity and training 
positive–negative margins, but **`run_full_diagnosis` does not invoke them**.

# Perturbation & Tests

Stage 1 compares clean inputs with three earlier-item permutations and three 
endpoint replacements. Stage 2 runs only when candidate scores change beyond 
numerical tolerance. At two intermediate residual sites, hooks replace only the 
readout-position activation: clean-to-corrupted restoration and 
corrupted-to-clean disruption, each compared with its own recipient baseline.

Other-user donors match original history length and endpoint ID. Their results 
are descriptive because donors may be reused. Controls check readout agreement, 
batch consistency, bounded future invariance, repeatability, activation 
layout/padding, and no-op/identity patches. Failed sites invalidate their patch 
results.

# MODEL_DIAGNOSIS Schema & Limitations

The return contains `metrics` and `metric_definitions`, including status, 
summaries, paired condition comparisons, donor summaries, controls, coverage, 
errors and replay-manifest location. The interpreter prompt requests a grounded 
JSON interpretation.

Measurements concern conditional training sensitivity, not host validation or 
generalization. Hybrid activation patches do not establish universal causality. 
Export parity, complete dependency paths, other temporal positions and 
unavailable metadata remain unverified. A 120-second category cap can leave 
partial results.

