Results of Code Analyze
# Model Overview (for Diagnosis)

The supplied code contains diagnosis logic only; the NextItNet backbone and 
final sequence readout are not shown. The probe accesses item embeddings through
`view.item_emb`, per-position sequence features through 
`view.log2feats(sequence)`, and candidate logits through 
`view.predict_score(features, items)`. It does not inspect or verify 
final-position readout behavior.

# Diagnosis Inputs & Hooks

`DiagnosisProbe(view, device)` stores the compatibility view as `self.model`, 
including `meta_data`, `review_data`, and `user_train`. Only metadata is 
subsequently queried; reviews and stored histories are unused.

The margin probe switches the view to evaluation mode and processes up to ten 
training batches under `no_grad`. Each batch supplies `(None, sequence, 
positive, negative)` with tensors shaped B × L. Sequence features and paired 
candidate IDs produce positive and negative logits at each position. Positions 
with positive ID zero are excluded.

# Probes & Metrics

- **Embedding collapse:** Samples up to 2,048 real item embeddings with seed 42,
excluding padding and reserved tokens. It normalizes embeddings, averages 
off-diagonal cosine similarities, and maps the result using `(mean_cosine + 1) /
2`. Higher values indicate greater directional alignment; approximately 0.5 
corresponds to mean cosine zero.
- **Positive–negative separation:** Computes `positive_logit − negative_logit`. 
Reports mean, tenth percentile, fraction below zero, and fraction below 0.5. 
Larger margins and smaller failure fractions generally indicate better 
separation of the sampled pairs.
- **Category breakdown:** Counts metadata categories separately for positive and
negative items in low-margin and wrong-ranking cases, retaining the top twenty 
categories per group.

# Perturbation & Tests

There are no sequence perturbations, shuffling tests, gradient probes, or SVD 
analyses. Sampling embeddings limits computation; positive–negative comparisons 
assess discrimination on existing training examples rather than sensitivity to 
history changes.

# MODEL_DIAGNOSIS Schema & Limitations

`run_full_diagnosis` returns `metrics` and `metric_definitions`, covering five 
scalar metrics and `pos_neg_margin_category_breakdown`. The interpreter function
embeds both dictionaries into a prompt requesting JSON with `status`, 
`core_findings`, `key_implications`, and `evidence`; it does not itself produce 
an interpretation.

Category metadata is unavailable in this task, so category lists should remain 
empty. Counts are unnormalized and do not establish category-specific error 
rates. These probes do not measure validation HR@10, temporal sensitivity, 
recommendation coverage, or user traits. Empty valid samples return zero margin 
metrics, which can misleadingly resemble healthy failure rates.

